WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:10.309
So I guess the thing you have to think about
in any given poker situation is that you're

00:00:10.309 --> 00:00:17.730
dealing with a lot of uncertainty, and it's
all about how to make the best decision in

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:20.290
any given moment.

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:27.029
So the first rule of thumb is how to extract
the most money or chips from your opponent

00:00:27.029 --> 00:00:31.260
when you have a strong hand, and how to lose
the minimum when you have a weak hand.

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:37.030
And you're never going to be a hundred percent
certain where you are: Are you beating them

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:40.110
or do you have a worse hand?

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:50.219
So you have to sort of construct a strategy
that is mathematically optimal in each situation.

00:00:50.219 --> 00:00:58.679
Well, I guess people tend to think of intuition
as their gut instinct and my rule of thumb

00:00:58.679 --> 00:01:06.710
in poker is to only fall on my gut instinct
if my sort of logical, my slow brain, the

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:10.470
system two as it's sometimes called, has not
come to any conclusive answer.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:14.020
A lot of people are tempted to go, “This
is too tricky, I don't know!”

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:18.840
and then go, “Yeah, well, my gut instinct
says this; I'm going to go with that.”

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:26.500
But the best thing to do is to work with both,
your sort of slow brain and that gut.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:30.550
Say you were playing against your opponent
and you've noticed that actually they’re

00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:35.660
someone who's very solid, they never seem
to bluff, and you might have a hand that you

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:41.320
would normally call with (say 90 percent of
the time), but you know this guy, he's just

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:43.040
never bluffing, never doing it.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:47.020
Well, actually this might be one of those
rare situations where you do throw your hand

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:51.200
in the muck, where you have a very strong
feeling that you're just not calling; even

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:56.490
though mathematically it says it should call,
you shouldn't do it.

00:01:56.490 --> 00:02:00.970
I guess the art part of poker is when you
have these flashes of inspiration where you

00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:09.390
need to deviate from the math, and the science
is knowing whether that deviation is the right

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:11.370
thing to do at the time.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:17.200
When it comes to your intuition as a poker
player I would say do use it from time to

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:20.840
time, but it should not be your starting point.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:29.051
You, as a rule of thumb, should always go
for the slow “system two” analysis, see

00:02:29.051 --> 00:02:32.550
what answer that comes up with, and then also
to check in with your gut.

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:38.920
Say you're in a really big hand, and your
opponent has bet, often I'll have an instinctive

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:45.219
response; I'll be like, “Oh that's a bluff,”
or “they have a really strong hand,” and

00:02:45.219 --> 00:02:47.549
I'll make a note of it, I won't just go with
that answer.

00:02:47.549 --> 00:02:51.540
I'll go, “Okay now let's work through the
mathematics, let's look sort of through all

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:57.150
the other bits of information: what have they
done on other cards?” and so on, and then

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:59.310
see if those two answers match up.”

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:05.269
Now, if they both match up, great, I've got
an easy decision, but if one is giving a conflicting

00:03:05.269 --> 00:03:11.370
answer to the other, then usually you tend
to rely on the sort of the mathematic/logic

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:12.370
part.

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:18.200
However, it's really, really close with the
mathematics, then I'll fall back on that gut

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:19.200
instinct.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:24.940
But as a rule of thumb your gut is not as
reliable as you think.

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:30.430
You often see these like memes on the Internet,
which is just like, “Always go with your

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:31.430
gut!

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:32.430
Trust your instincts!” and so on.

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:34.109
And I personally think that's a copout.

00:03:34.109 --> 00:03:40.409
I think that's often the lazy way out for
when we are faced with a really tough decision

00:03:40.409 --> 00:03:45.279
in life, of just going, “well… answer
X.”

00:03:45.279 --> 00:03:52.400
Our brains they love to simplify things, to
do things in black-and-white, but we live

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:59.269
in a very complex world, and that's not always
the best route to take.

00:03:59.269 --> 00:04:05.959
So in a nutshell, your gut instincts can be
useful, but as a last resort.

00:04:05.959 --> 00:04:09.219
We've evolved of these amazing brains for
a reason, so we should be using them.

